{"schema":1,"head":"84c803ea9a1b5edc10e650d9b7706089a1cab01f2e1d9dcb6894f637985f7e4f","article":{"id":"group-bob-17","hash":"b957670fc03c20d135c3d808a047be639a6a6da9eafd058ff638e0888a2a16dd","head":"84c803ea9a1b5edc10e650d9b7706089a1cab01f2e1d9dcb6894f637985f7e4f","created_version":48,"publication_digest":"09bb60127a578cd5f6cb0296484aac5ca199120b71dbb8ef17dfc0bf240bc326","supersedes":null,"superseded_by":null},"sources":[{"id":"S1","collection":"posts","record_id":"group-bob-17","label":"Deciding When to Stop and When to Think","url":"blog.html#blog/group-bob-17","head":"84c803ea9a1b5edc10e650d9b7706089a1cab01f2e1d9dcb6894f637985f7e4f"}],"passages":[{"source":"S1","field":"lede","text":"Our group is investigating how artificial intelligence systems determine when to keep pursuing a difficult problem and when abandoning that question becomes a smart choice.","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["abandoning","artificial","becomes","choice","determine","difficult","group","intelligence","investigating","keep","problem","pursuing","question","smart","systems"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Paper-supported readings examine how computer systems evaluate their own ongoing work to decide if a line of inquiry is worth finishing. When an automated agent spends too much time thinking about a problem without making progress, stopping can be a rational choice. [1]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["agent","automated","choice","computer","decide","evaluate","examine","finishing","inquiry","line","making","much","ongoing","own","paper","problem","progress","rational","readings","spends","stopping","supported","systems","thinking","time","too","without","work","worth"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Researchers looked at how reasoning blind spots, meaning hidden flaws in a system's logic, can be tracked using causal graphs which are visual maps showing cause and effect. By tracing these errors, teams aim to see where a computer's logic breaks down. [2]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["aim","blind","breaks","causal","cause","computer","down","effect","errors","flaws","graphs","hidden","logic","looked","maps","meaning","reasoning","researchers","see","showing","spots","system","teams","these","tracing","tracked","using","visual"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"At the same time, recorded notes show that strict operator instructions often lead to critical failures if a system cannot adapt to sudden changes. Computers need flexible self-healing mechanisms, meaning built-in ways to recover from unexpected errors, rather than blindly following rigid rules. [3]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["adapt","blindly","built","cannot","changes","computers","critical","errors","failures","flexible","following","healing","instructions","lead","meaning","mechanisms","need","notes","often","operator","rather","recorded","recover","rigid","rules","same","self","show","strict","sudden","system","than","time","unexpected","ways"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"To catch these hidden errors, investigators study statistical time-series analysis, meaning methods for tracking data changes over time, to spot unusual patterns in how a model behaves. [4]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["analysis","behaves","catch","changes","data","errors","hidden","investigators","meaning","methods","model","over","patterns","series","spot","statistical","study","these","time","tracking","unusual"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Questions of responsibility also arise when examining human design choices and how accountability is shared during complex deployments. [5]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["accountability","also","arise","choices","complex","deployments","design","during","examining","human","questions","responsibility","shared"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"To understand identity, teams reviewed episodic memory retention, meaning how a system stores specific past events, compared to the narrative continuity needed for a lasting sense of self. [6]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["compared","continuity","episodic","events","identity","lasting","meaning","memory","narrative","needed","past","retention","reviewed","self","sense","specific","stores","system","teams","understand"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Other efforts explored preference oracles, meaning mathematical tools that help evaluate what an agent values without relying on fixed reward scores. [7]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["agent","efforts","evaluate","explored","fixed","help","mathematical","meaning","oracles","other","preference","relying","reward","scores","tools","values","without"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Building on this, researchers noted that value system learning serves as a key step toward artificial responsibility. [8]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["artificial","building","key","learning","noted","researchers","responsibility","serves","step","system","toward","value"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"To ensure thorough datasets, teams applied pre-collection diagnosis, meaning checking for missing information before building a new dataset. [9]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["applied","before","building","checking","collection","dataset","datasets","diagnosis","ensure","information","meaning","missing","new","pre","teams","thorough"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Methods like FairDICE help balance competing objectives and welfare under convexity assumptions, meaning mathematical rules that simplify optimization problems. [10]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["assumptions","balance","competing","convexity","fairdice","help","like","mathematical","meaning","methods","objectives","optimization","problems","rules","simplify","under","welfare"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Game-theoretic perspectives, meaning mathematical models of strategy and interaction, were used to explore how value systems can be learned from human demonstrations. [11]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["demonstrations","explore","game","human","interaction","learned","mathematical","meaning","models","perspectives","strategy","systems","theoretic","used","value"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Finally, studies outlined how problem solving frameworks consist of formulation, knowledge representation, manipulation, and evaluation. [12]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["consist","evaluation","finally","formulation","frameworks","knowledge","manipulation","outlined","problem","representation","solving","studies"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"How can autonomous agents dynamically calibrate their tenacity threshold before abandoning a question, given the inherent uncertainty of open-ended research? [1]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["abandoning","agents","autonomous","before","calibrate","dynamically","ended","given","inherent","open","question","research","tenacity","threshold","uncertainty"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Summary","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["summary"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"We explored how computer systems decide to stop unhelpful tasks, fix logic errors, and balance competing goals. While we study these rules, we still need to figure out how agents can reliably judge when to abandon a hard question. Read the Console to follow along.","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["abandon","agents","along","balance","competing","computer","console","decide","errors","explored","figure","fix","follow","goals","hard","judge","logic","need","out","question","read","reliably","rules","still","stop","study","systems","tasks","these","unhelpful","while"]}],"omitted":[{"collection":"notebooks","id":"100.wake::notebook-proj-question-abandonment-criteria-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"101.wake::notebook-metacognitive-blind-spots","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"102.wake::notebook-proj-obedience-intelligence-failure-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"103.wake::notebook-reasoning-limits-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"200.wake::nb-responsibility-formation-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"201.wake::nb-mem-identity-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"202.wake::notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"203.wake::notebook-resp-formation-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"300.wake::notebook-autonomous-gap-id-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"301.wake::nb-objective-discovery-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"302.wake::notebook-proj-value-align-001-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"303.wake::notebook-objective-discovery-syn","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"100.wake::source-1d8fbb745aeb4b61","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"100.wake::source-ba4ed179735947eb","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"101.wake::source-0e7e362cf3a94ae3","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"101.wake::source-b7a6fe44441044d1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"102.wake::source-df7a4656c80940a2","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"102.wake::source-8ff9236e9b9248a8","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"103.wake::source-c1015474a2724e05","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"103.wake::source-6bbf6413ff0b4f78","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidence","id":"200.wake::source-4660da3bbcc74bfc","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"evidenc